.TH tucsplit n 0.2 tclutils
.SH tclutils::tucsplit
.PP
Split text files by content markers, similar in spirit to \fBcsplit\fR.
.SS Load
.CS
package require tclutils::tucsplit
.CE
.SS Example
.CS
set files [::tclutils::tucsplit::file handbook.md {^# } -outdir chapters -prefix chapter -suffix .md]
.CE
.PP
By default, a matching line starts the next part and is kept in that part.
.SS Commands
.IP \(bu
\fBsplitText text pattern ?options?\fR
.IP \(bu
\fBfile path pattern ?options?\fR
.PP
Options include \fB\-regexp\fR, \fB\-keepmatch\fR, \fB\-prefix\fR, \fB\-suffix\fR, \fB\-outdir\fR, \fB\-digits\fR, \fB\-empty\fR and \fB\-encoding\fR.
.PP
Defaults:
.CS
-regexp 1      pattern is a regular expression (0: string match)
-keepmatch 1   the matching line starts the new part
-empty 0       do not write empty parts
-prefix xx     parts are named xx00, xx01, ...
-suffix ""     no suffix
-digits 2      digits of the running number
-outdir .      target directory, created if missing
-encoding utf-8  encoding of the parts; file reads in it too
.CE
.PP
Every line keeps its line end, so the parts put together give the input again, as with \fBcsplit\fR (0.2; 0.1 ended each part without \fB\en\fR). With \fB\-keepmatch 0\fR the matching lines are left out. \fBfile\fR reads the bytes in \fB\-encoding\fR and keeps CRLF; a pattern is matched against the line without its line end. An unknown encoding raises \fBTCLUTILS TUCSPLIT ENCODING name\fR.
